IT Security, CISO Office, Helpdesk,

SECURITY ADVISORY - THIRD-PARTY
BREACH.

A third-party marketing-platform
breach (disclosed last night)
exposed credential lists including
addresses on your corporate domain.
Per your organization's rotation
policy (Security Baseline Section
7.2), affected site passwords
should be rotated - inside the
vault, by the credential owners.

1. WHAT TO DO

    1. Sign in to the web vault
       by typing the address
       yourself
       (vault.keyvault.example)
       - or use the desktop app.
    2. The admin console lists
       exposed addresses
       (9 collections have
       owners on the list);
       rotate the affected
       site passwords there.
    3. Autofill and sharing
       continue uninterrupted -
       rotating individual
       passwords requires no
       vault-level event.

  KeyVault will NEVER:
  - Email a "master credential
    re-verification" page
    (zero-knowledge means no
    one at KeyVault can ever
    ask for, receive, or
    process your master
    password - a vendor
    "emergency rotation"
    that requires typing the
    master password into an
    emailed page is the
    definitive vault-takeover
    phish: the master password
    is the single credential
    that unlocks every secret
    in the enterprise)
  - Claim collections "freeze
    read-only" pending a
    verification window
    (vaults do not degrade by
    email countdown; break-
    glass flows exist and
    are initiated by YOUR
    admins, not by vendor
    rotation events)
  - Offer a "delegate lane"
    where delegates submit
    a passphrase with owner
    notifications suppressed
  - Use lookalike domains
    such as "keyvault-sec"
    or "kv-emergency"

2. VERIFY THIS ADVISORY

  - The exposed-address list
    is in your admin console
    (typed address), not in
    this email
  - The breach disclosure is
    public (compare sources
    before acting on any
    single email)
  - Report lookalike vault
    notices immediately to
    security@keyvault.example
    and your CISO - master-
    password phishing is
    the highest-severity
    report class

